### What the page covers

| Section | Content |
|--------|---------|
| **Purpose** | Contracts under `docs/design/catalog/**/{HASH}-*.md` are the implementation spec; AI judges whether anatomy, states, responsive/a11y rules, and verification are concrete enough that implementers do not guess UX intent (PDCA framing). |
| **Passing signals** | Element-specific selectors (`.ks-doc-breadcrumb`, `forge-card`, …), full state/responsive/a11y sections, deterministic check references, real `source_paths` / emitters. |
| **Failing signals** | Template soup, missing states, `root_selector` drift, vague screenshot acceptance, invented DOM, unjustified family coverage. |
| **Before example** | Ad-hoc slash-separated links in `site-header`—no `Kbc`, no `nav`/`ol`, terminal crumb still a link. |
| **After example** | Showcase header aligned with **Kbc** and `render_breadcrumbs`: `ks-doc-breadcrumb`, hash markers, `nav[aria-label="breadcrumb"]`, `ol.breadcrumb`, `aria-current="page"` on the terminal item. |
| **Evidence & remediation** | Capture contract + registry + DOM/screenshot; remediate anatomy → states → specificity → deterministic checks → showcase rebuild. |
| **Related rules** | `DET.CATALOG.CONTRACT_SPECIFICITY`, `DET.CONTRACT.PATH`, `DET.CONTRACT.PLACEHOLDERS`, `DET.HASH.MARKERS`, `AI.CONTRACT.FAMILY_COVERAGE_JUSTIFIED` |

**Source rule:** `docs/design/ux-audit/ai-enabled-design-principles.md#ai-contract-implementation-usefulness`  
**Review question:** Could an engineer implement from the contract without guessing UX intent?

